"use strict";(self.webpackChunknetworkmanager=self.webpackChunknetworkmanager||[]).push([["8725"],{5514(e){e.exports=JSON.parse('{"archive":{"blogPosts":[{"id":"release-highlights","metadata":{"permalink":"/NETworkManager/blog/release-highlights","editUrl":"https://github.com/bornToBeRoot/NETworkManager/tree/main/Website/blog/2026-11-15-release-highlights/index.md","source":"@site/blog/2026-11-15-release-highlights/index.md","title":"Run Command, Visual Traceroute, a Smarter IP Scanner, RDAP and a Fresh Look","description":"NETworkManager 2026.11.15.0 brings a run command to start any tool from the keyboard, a world map for Traceroute, an IP Scanner that finds and identifies far more devices, RDAP as the modern successor of Whois, and a redesigned interface with new themes - plus hundreds of improvements and bug fixes.","date":"2026-11-15T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"release","permalink":"/NETworkManager/blog/tags/release"},{"inline":true,"label":"run command","permalink":"/NETworkManager/blog/tags/run-command"},{"inline":true,"label":"traceroute","permalink":"/NETworkManager/blog/tags/traceroute"},{"inline":true,"label":"ip scanner","permalink":"/NETworkManager/blog/tags/ip-scanner"},{"inline":true,"label":"rdap","permalink":"/NETworkManager/blog/tags/rdap"},{"inline":true,"label":"theme","permalink":"/NETworkManager/blog/tags/theme"},{"inline":true,"label":"ui","permalink":"/NETworkManager/blog/tags/ui"},{"inline":true,"label":"new feature","permalink":"/NETworkManager/blog/tags/new-feature"}],"readingTime":6.3,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"BornToBeRoot","title":"Maintainer of NETworkManager","url":"https://github.com/BornToBeRoot","imageURL":"https://github.com/BornToBeRoot.png","key":"borntoberoot","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"release-highlights","title":"Run Command, Visual Traceroute, a Smarter IP Scanner, RDAP and a Fresh Look","description":"NETworkManager 2026.11.15.0 brings a run command to start any tool from the keyboard, a world map for Traceroute, an IP Scanner that finds and identifies far more devices, RDAP as the modern successor of Whois, and a redesigned interface with new themes - plus hundreds of improvements and bug fixes.","authors":["borntoberoot"],"tags":["release","run command","traceroute","ip scanner","rdap","theme","ui","new feature"],"keywords":["NETworkManager","release","what\'s new","run command","command palette","visual traceroute","traceroute map","IP Scanner","network discovery","device detection","RDAP","Whois","theme","dark theme","redesign","UI"]},"unlisted":false,"nextItem":{"title":"Introducing RDAP","permalink":"/NETworkManager/blog/introducing-rdap"}},"content":"The new release of NETworkManager (version **2026.11.15.0**) is one of the biggest so far: a **run command** to start any tool from the keyboard, a **world map** for Traceroute, an **IP Scanner** that finds and identifies far more devices, the new **RDAP** tool, and a **redesigned interface** with new themes and accent colors. On top of that, it contains more than 150 improvements and more than 150 bug fixes across all tools.\\n\\n:::note[Placeholder]\\n\\nPreview image of the new design (e.g. main window with the IP Scanner, dark theme).\\n\\n:::\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\n## Run Command\\n\\nPress `Ctrl+Shift+P`, type what you want to do and press `Enter` - NETworkManager opens the right tool and runs it:\\n\\n```\\nping 10.0.0.1\\ndns borntoberoot.net 1.1.1.1 aaaa\\nportscan 10.0.0.1 webserver\\nrdp @server-01\\nsubnet 10.0.0.0/16 /24\\n```\\n\\n- **Short aliases** for every tool (e.g. `ping`, `dns`, `rdp`, `ssh`, `geo`, `wol`) and **profiles** with `@` (e.g. `rdp @server-01`).\\n- **Suggestions** while you type: recent commands, profiles, port profiles and types - `Tab` completes, `Up`/`Down` selects.\\n- **Just a target** is enough: type `10.0.0.1`, `server-01` or `00:11:22:33:44:55` and get the commands that accept it. Optionally, a target in the clipboard is suggested.\\n- The input is **validated like in the tool** itself, and the dialog shows how it was understood (e.g. `Hosts: borntoberoot.net`, `Type: AAAA`).\\n- Open it from the **Run command...** box in the title bar, or from anywhere in Windows with an optional **global hotkey** (`Ctrl+Alt+P`).\\n\\n![Run command](../../docs/img/run-command.gif)\\n\\nAll commands and their syntax are listed in the [Run Command documentation](https://borntoberoot.net/NETworkManager/docs/run-command).\\n\\n## Visual Traceroute\\n\\nTraceroute now shows the route on a **world map** below the hop list. Each hop with a resolved geolocation gets a marker, consecutive hops are connected with curved arrows in the direction of the trace. Hover a marker to see its location, ISP / AS number, hostname, IP address and average round-trip time. Zoom with the mouse wheel, drag to pan, or collapse the map if you don\'t need it.\\n\\nThe map works offline and is shown when **Check IP geolocation** and **Show map** are enabled.\\n\\n![Traceroute with the route on the world map](../../docs/img/traceroute.png)\\n\\nMore details in the [Traceroute documentation](https://borntoberoot.net/NETworkManager/docs/application/traceroute#map).\\n\\n## A smarter IP Scanner\\n\\nThe IP Scanner received the largest update in this release. It finds more devices, tells you what they are, and can watch a network for changes.\\n\\n**Find more hosts** - a host is found by whichever probe answers first: ping, open or refused TCP ports, NetBIOS, SNMP, or - in the local subnet - its ARP / NDP entry. Devices that block ping and all ports (e.g. a Windows client in a public network, a phone or an IoT device) are found as well. The tooltip of the status shows how a host was detected (e.g. `Up (detected by Ping, TCP)`).\\n\\n**Know what you found** - optional probes fill in what the device is:\\n\\n- **OS** guessed from the TTL (`Windows`, `Linux / Unix`, `Network device`), or the exact Windows version (e.g. `Windows 11 25H2`) with **Read Windows host information** (beta) - including domain, time offset and logged-on users.\\n- **mDNS names** (`.local`) of Apple devices, printers and IoT devices.\\n- **Web pages**: title, server and TLS certificate of routers, printers and NAS.\\n- **SNMP** name, description and location of switches and printers (`v1` / `v2c`, `v3` via profiles).\\n- **Roles**: this computer, the default gateway and the DNS and DHCP servers are marked.\\n\\n**Watch the network** - every scan is compared with the previous one: hosts that are **New**, **Gone** or have a **changed MAC address** are highlighted. **Automatically scan every** repeats the scan (`1 min` to `1 h`) and shows a notification when something changed.\\n\\n**Faster and easier to use** - an empty `/24` network is scanned in about 8 seconds instead of about 50, hosts appear as soon as they answer, and results can be searched while the scan is running. Exclude addresses with `!` (e.g. `10.0.0.0/24; !10.0.0.1`), scan all local subnets with one click, and rescan single hosts from the context menu.\\n\\n![IP Scanner - Windows host information of a domain controller](../../docs/img/ip-scanner-windows-host-infos.png)\\n\\nReading the Windows host information with credentials needs care in a domain - learn how NETworkManager prevents account lockouts in the [FAQ](https://borntoberoot.net/NETworkManager/docs/faq/windows-account-lockout-protection). All probes and settings are explained in the [IP Scanner documentation](https://borntoberoot.net/NETworkManager/docs/application/ip-scanner#how-hosts-are-found).\\n\\n## RDAP\\n\\nThe new **RDAP** tool looks up who a domain, an IP address, an AS number or an organization is registered to - via the Registration Data Access Protocol, the standardized successor of Whois. Results are structured (details, events, nameservers, DNSSEC, contacts), come over HTTPS, and NETworkManager finds the responsible registry automatically, including the referral to the registrar for domains like `.com`. If a registry only offers Whois, the domain opens in the **Whois** tool with one click.\\n\\n![RDAP](../../docs/img/rdap.png)\\n\\nRDAP is also available from the context menu of the **IP Scanner** and **Traceroute** (e.g. the AS number of a hop) and from the run command (`rdap borntoberoot.net`).\\n\\nLearn more in the deep dive [Introducing RDAP](https://borntoberoot.net/NETworkManager/blog/introducing-rdap) and the [RDAP documentation](https://borntoberoot.net/NETworkManager/docs/application/rdap).\\n\\n## A fresh look\\n\\nThe whole interface was redesigned for a calmer, more modern look - without changing where things are:\\n\\n- **New themes**: Black, Midnight, Steel and Indigo (dark), Mist, Soft and Paper (light) - and **new accent colors** like Sky, Ocean, Mint, Rose, Coral, Gold, Lavender, Slate and Graphite. Every accent works with every theme.\\n- **New layout**: navigation, tool and profiles as rounded boxes on a tinted window frame, tabs like in VS Code, rounded windows on Windows 11, and an optional **accent glow**.\\n- **One icon family** ([Lucide](https://lucide.dev)) instead of five icon packs, thin-line icons with the same size everywhere.\\n- **Easier to read**: Segoe UI Variable on Windows 11, technical values (IP / MAC addresses, ports, hostnames) in Cascadia Mono, clearer status colors in light and dark themes.\\n- **Subtle animations** for dialogs, navigation and expanders - following the Windows setting, or turned off in the settings or for all users with a [system-wide policy](https://borntoberoot.net/NETworkManager/blog/system-wide-policies-for-enterprise-deployments).\\n\\n![Dashboard in the new design](../../docs/img/dashboard.png)\\n\\nChoose your theme and accent in [Settings > Appearance](https://borntoberoot.net/NETworkManager/docs/settings/appearance).\\n\\n## More highlights\\n\\n- **WiFi**: see new, gone and changed access points since the last scan, with notifications.\\n- **Tabs**: dynamic tab width, and dragged-out tabs can be dropped anywhere on a tab bar - a hint shows where.\\n- **DNS**: a list of DNS servers with ports, and a DNS suffix for bare hostnames in all tools.\\n- **ARM64** builds are available.\\n\\n## Hundreds of improvements and bug fixes\\n\\nBesides the highlights, this release contains more than 150 improvements and more than 150 bug fixes - from faster scans and consistent exports to many small details in every tool. The complete list is in the [changelog](https://borntoberoot.net/NETworkManager/docs/changelog/2026-11-15-0).\\n\\n:::warning[Breaking changes]\\n\\nThe exports (CSV, XML, JSON) of all tools now use the same format: columns may have been reordered or added, and values are formatted consistently (e.g. numbers without unit, dates as `yyyy-MM-dd HH:mm:ss.fff`). If you process exports with scripts, check them after the update - see [Export format](https://borntoberoot.net/NETworkManager/docs/faq/export-format).\\n\\n:::\\n\\n## Try it now\\n\\nDownload the [latest version of NETworkManager](https://borntoberoot.net/NETworkManager/download).\\n\\nIf you find any issues or have suggestions, please open an [issue on GitHub](https://github.com/BornToBeRoot/NETworkManager/issues)."},{"id":"introducing-rdap","metadata":{"permalink":"/NETworkManager/blog/introducing-rdap","editUrl":"https://github.com/bornToBeRoot/NETworkManager/tree/main/Website/blog/2026-10-11-introducing-rdap/index.md","source":"@site/blog/2026-10-11-introducing-rdap/index.md","title":"Introducing RDAP","description":"NETworkManager introduces RDAP, the modern successor of Whois. Look up registration data for domains, IP addresses, AS numbers and entities - structured, over HTTPS and with the right server found automatically.","date":"2026-10-11T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"rdap","permalink":"/NETworkManager/blog/tags/rdap"},{"inline":true,"label":"whois","permalink":"/NETworkManager/blog/tags/whois"},{"inline":true,"label":"domain","permalink":"/NETworkManager/blog/tags/domain"},{"inline":true,"label":"ip address","permalink":"/NETworkManager/blog/tags/ip-address"},{"inline":true,"label":"asn","permalink":"/NETworkManager/blog/tags/asn"},{"inline":true,"label":"new feature","permalink":"/NETworkManager/blog/tags/new-feature"}],"readingTime":5.25,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"BornToBeRoot","title":"Maintainer of NETworkManager","url":"https://github.com/BornToBeRoot","imageURL":"https://github.com/BornToBeRoot.png","key":"borntoberoot","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"introducing-rdap","title":"Introducing RDAP","description":"NETworkManager introduces RDAP, the modern successor of Whois. Look up registration data for domains, IP addresses, AS numbers and entities - structured, over HTTPS and with the right server found automatically.","authors":["borntoberoot"],"tags":["rdap","whois","domain","ip address","asn","new feature"],"keywords":["NETworkManager","RDAP","Registration Data Access Protocol","Whois","Whois alternative","domain lookup","IP lookup","ASN lookup","AS number","registrar","registry","IANA bootstrap"]},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Run Command, Visual Traceroute, a Smarter IP Scanner, RDAP and a Fresh Look","permalink":"/NETworkManager/blog/release-highlights"},"nextItem":{"title":"Import Profiles from Active Directory","permalink":"/NETworkManager/blog/import-profiles-from-active-directory"}},"content":"NETworkManager introduces a new tool, `RDAP`. With the **Registration Data Access Protocol** you can look up who a domain, an IP address, an AS number or an organization is registered to - with structured results, over HTTPS, and without having to know which server to ask.\\n\\n![RDAP](../../docs/img/rdap.png)\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\n## What is RDAP?\\n\\nRDAP is the standardized successor of Whois ([RFC 7480](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc7480), [RFC 9082](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9082), [RFC 9083](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9083), [RFC 9224](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9224)). It answers the same questions as Whois - who registered this domain, which organization owns this IP range, which network is behind this AS number - but fixes most of its shortcomings:\\n\\n| | Whois | RDAP |\\n| --- | --- | --- |\\n| **Transport** | Plain text over TCP port 43 | HTTPS over TCP port 443 (a few registries still only offer HTTP) |\\n| **Response format** | Free text, different for every registry | Standardized JSON |\\n| **Finding the server** | Hard-coded list of servers per TLD | Official IANA bootstrap files |\\n| **Lookups** | Mainly domains | Domains, IP addresses/networks, AS numbers, entities |\\n| **Internationalized domain names** | Inconsistent | Supported |\\n\\nRDAP is mandatory for all generic top-level domains (e.g. `.com`, `.net`, `.org`) and is provided by all five Regional Internet Registries (ARIN, RIPE NCC, APNIC, LACNIC, AFRINIC). Since January 2025, gTLD registries and registrars are no longer required to operate Whois at all - RDAP is the way forward.\\n\\n## What you can do with it\\n\\nEnter a query and press **Query** - the type is detected automatically (**Auto**), or you select it, e.g. to query a nameserver:\\n\\n| Type | Example | Description |\\n| --- | --- | --- |\\n| **Domain** | `borntoberoot.net` | Registration data of a domain from its registry |\\n| **Nameserver** | `a.iana-servers.net` | A nameserver, from the registry of its top-level domain |\\n| **TLD** | `de` | Delegation of a top-level domain from the IANA root zone |\\n| **IP address** | `1.1.1.1` or `2001:db8::/32` | Network an IP address or CIDR prefix belongs to |\\n| **ASN** | `AS13335` | Autonomous system - `13335`, `AS13335` and asdot (`1.10`) work |\\n| **Entity** | `ORG-RIEN1-RIPE` | An organization or contact, by its handle |\\n\\nThe input is validated while you type, depending on the selected type. For a hostname like `www.borntoberoot.net` or `www.bbc.co.uk`, the registered domain (`borntoberoot.net`, `bbc.co.uk`) is queried - registries don\'t know subdomains. NETworkManager determines it with the included [Public Suffix List](https://publicsuffix.org/). A running query can be canceled with **Cancel** or `Esc`.\\n\\nThe result is shown in a structured view:\\n\\n- **Details** like handle, name, address range, country, status and DNSSEC\\n- **Events** such as registration, expiration and last change\\n- **Nameservers** including their IP addresses\\n- **Contacts** with roles, name, organization, email, phone and address\\n- **Networks** and **AS numbers** of an organization (e.g. at RIPE NCC)\\n- **Notices** with links to the terms of service, privacy policy or the form to report inaccurate data\\n\\nIf you need everything the server sent, switch to **Raw JSON**. Results can be exported as **JSON** (exactly as returned by the server) or as **text**, via the **Export...** button or the context menu.\\n\\n## How it works\\n\\nYou don\'t need to know which server is responsible for a query - NETworkManager finds it for you:\\n\\n1. **Bootstrap**: IANA publishes bootstrap files that map TLDs, IP ranges, AS numbers and entity tags to the responsible RDAP servers. NETworkManager downloads them with the first query, caches them locally and checks for updates once a week. You can see their status and update them manually in **Settings > RDAP**.\\n2. **Query**: The query is sent to the responsible registry - via HTTPS, plain HTTP is only used if a registry offers nothing else. Redirects between registries (e.g. from ARIN to RIPE NCC) are followed automatically.\\n3. **Referral**: For many domains (e.g. `.com` and `.net`), the registry only returns basic data and refers to the RDAP server of the registrar for the details. NETworkManager follows this referral and shows both results - registry and registrar - one below the other. You can disable this in the settings.\\n\\nA few details make the tool work in places where a plain bootstrap lookup would fail:\\n\\n- Some registries run an RDAP server that is not (yet) listed by IANA, for example `.de`, `.ch`, `.li` and `.io`. These are included in NETworkManager, so you get results for them as well.\\n- Private and reserved IP addresses like `10.0.0.1` or `fe80::1` return the IANA reservation instead of an error.\\n- If `data.iana.org` cannot be reached (e.g. in a restricted network), the cached files are used and the update is retried later.\\n\\nLike the other tools, RDAP supports **profiles**: store a query and its type in a profile and run it from the profile panel with a double-click.\\n\\nRDAP is also built into the other tools:\\n\\n- **IP Scanner**: query the IP address or the domain of a host from the context menu (**RDAP...**).\\n- **Traceroute**: query the IP address, hostname or AS number of a hop from the context menu - e.g. to find out who operates a hop on the route.\\n- **Run command**: press `Ctrl+Shift+P` and type e.g. `rdap borntoberoot.net` or `rdap 1.10 asn`.\\n\\n## RDAP and Whois\\n\\nNot every registry offers RDAP yet. Many country code top-level domains (e.g. `.ru`, `.jp` or `.it`) still only provide Whois. If no RDAP server is known for a domain, NETworkManager tells you so and offers to open the domain in the **Whois** tool with a single click.\\n\\nKeep in mind that RDAP does not unlock any data that Whois hides: registration data of personal contacts is usually redacted for privacy reasons (GDPR), with both protocols.\\n\\n## Why it\'s useful\\n\\n- **Find the owner of an IP address or network** - e.g. when analyzing logs, firewall hits or abuse reports - including the abuse contact of the responsible organization.\\n- **Check domains**: registrar, status (e.g. transfer locks), expiration date, nameservers and DNSSEC in one view.\\n- **Look up AS numbers** to see which organization operates a network, e.g. when troubleshooting routing or peering.\\n- **One tool for all registries**: no need to know whether an address belongs to ARIN, RIPE NCC or APNIC, or which registrar manages a domain.\\n- **Structured and exportable**: copy single values or export the full JSON for documentation and further processing.\\n\\n## Try it now\\n\\nRDAP is available in the [latest version of NETworkManager](https://borntoberoot.net/NETworkManager/download).\\n\\nMore details are available in the [official documentation](https://borntoberoot.net/NETworkManager/docs/application/rdap).\\n\\nIf you find any issues or have suggestions, please open an [issue on GitHub](https://github.com/BornToBeRoot/NETworkManager/issues)."},{"id":"import-profiles-from-active-directory","metadata":{"permalink":"/NETworkManager/blog/import-profiles-from-active-directory","editUrl":"https://github.com/bornToBeRoot/NETworkManager/tree/main/Website/blog/2026-05-18-import-profiles-from-active-directory/index.md","source":"@site/blog/2026-05-18-import-profiles-from-active-directory/index.md","title":"Import Profiles from Active Directory","description":"NETworkManager now lets you import profiles directly from Active Directory. Query computer accounts via LDAP, review the results, and bulk-import them as profiles in seconds.","date":"2026-05-18T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"profiles","permalink":"/NETworkManager/blog/tags/profiles"},{"inline":true,"label":"active directory","permalink":"/NETworkManager/blog/tags/active-directory"},{"inline":true,"label":"import","permalink":"/NETworkManager/blog/tags/import"},{"inline":true,"label":"new feature","permalink":"/NETworkManager/blog/tags/new-feature"}],"readingTime":2.32,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"BornToBeRoot","title":"Maintainer of NETworkManager","url":"https://github.com/BornToBeRoot","imageURL":"https://github.com/BornToBeRoot.png","key":"borntoberoot","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"import-profiles-from-active-directory","title":"Import Profiles from Active Directory","description":"NETworkManager now lets you import profiles directly from Active Directory. Query computer accounts via LDAP, review the results, and bulk-import them as profiles in seconds.","authors":["borntoberoot"],"tags":["profiles","active directory","import","new feature"],"keywords":["NETworkManager","profile import","Active Directory","LDAP","bulk import","computer accounts","sysadmin"]},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Introducing RDAP","permalink":"/NETworkManager/blog/introducing-rdap"},"nextItem":{"title":"Introducing Neighbor Table","permalink":"/NETworkManager/blog/introducing-neighbor-table"}},"content":"NETworkManager now lets you import profiles directly from **Active Directory**. Instead of creating profiles for every machine by hand, you can query your AD for computer accounts via LDAP, review the results, and bulk-import them into any group with a single click.\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\n## The problem with large environments\\n\\nIf you manage dozens or hundreds of machines, maintaining NETworkManager profiles manually is tedious. You have to add each host one by one, set the display name, pick the right group, and enable the applications you need. When machines are added or decommissioned in AD, keeping profiles in sync becomes its own maintenance burden.\\n\\nThe new **Active Directory import** solves this by pulling computer accounts directly from your domain.\\n\\n## How it works\\n\\nGo to **Settings \u2192 Profiles** and click **Import**. Select **Active Directory** as the import source. A dialog opens where you configure the LDAP connection:\\n\\n![Profiles - Import Active Directory](./profiles--import-active-directory.png)\\n\\n| Field | Description |\\n| --- | --- |\\n| **Search base** | LDAP search base, e.g. `DC=domain,DC=com` |\\n| **Server** | Hostname or IP of the LDAP server. Leave empty to use the default domain controller. |\\n| **Port** | Defaults to `389` (LDAP) or `636` (LDAPS), switches automatically when **Use SSL** is toggled. |\\n| **Use SSL** | Connect via LDAPS (encrypted). |\\n| **Authentication** | Use current Windows credentials or supply a custom username and password. |\\n| **Exclude disabled accounts** | Skip computer accounts that are disabled in AD. |\\n| **Additional LDAP filter** | Narrow results further, e.g. `(operatingSystem=Windows Server*)`. |\\n\\nClick **Search** and NETworkManager queries the directory. All matching computer accounts appear in the next step.\\n\\n## Review and import\\n\\nBefore anything is written, you get a full review of what will be imported:\\n\\n![Profiles - Import Review](./profiles--import-review.png)\\n\\nEach entry shows a **Status** so you know exactly what will happen:\\n\\n- **New** \u2014 not yet in NETworkManager, will be imported.\\n- **Already imported** \u2014 already exists as a profile from this source. Skipped by default (configurable).\\n- **No host** \u2014 no host address available, cannot be imported.\\n\\nOn the right side you choose which **applications** to enable for the imported profiles. [Ping Monitor](https://borntoberoot.net/NETworkManager/docs/application/ping-monitor), [Remote Desktop](https://borntoberoot.net/NETworkManager/docs/application/remote-desktop), and [PowerShell](https://borntoberoot.net/NETworkManager/docs/application/powershell) are pre-selected, but you can change this before importing.\\n\\nYou also pick a **target group** \u2014 either an existing one or a new name you type in on the spot.\\n\\nClick **Import** and a summary shows how many profiles were created, skipped as duplicates, and skipped due to missing host addresses.\\n\\n## Try it now\\n\\nThe Active Directory import is available in the [latest version of NETworkManager](https://borntoberoot.net/NETworkManager/download).\\n\\nMore information is available in the [official documentation](https://borntoberoot.net/NETworkManager/docs/groups-and-profiles#import).\\n\\nIf you find any issues or have suggestions for improvement, please open an [issue on GitHub](https://github.com/BornToBeRoot/NETworkManager/issues)."},{"id":"introducing-neighbor-table","metadata":{"permalink":"/NETworkManager/blog/introducing-neighbor-table","editUrl":"https://github.com/bornToBeRoot/NETworkManager/tree/main/Website/blog/2026-05-03-introducing-neighbor-table/index.md","source":"@site/blog/2026-05-03-introducing-neighbor-table/index.md","title":"Introducing Neighbor Table","description":"NETworkManager introduces the Neighbor Table, replacing the ARP Table with a unified view that covers both IPv4 (ARP) and IPv6 (NDP) address-to-MAC mappings.","date":"2026-05-03T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"neighbor table","permalink":"/NETworkManager/blog/tags/neighbor-table"},{"inline":true,"label":"arp","permalink":"/NETworkManager/blog/tags/arp"},{"inline":true,"label":"ndp","permalink":"/NETworkManager/blog/tags/ndp"},{"inline":true,"label":"ipv6","permalink":"/NETworkManager/blog/tags/ipv-6"},{"inline":true,"label":"refactoring","permalink":"/NETworkManager/blog/tags/refactoring"}],"readingTime":2.43,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"BornToBeRoot","title":"Maintainer of NETworkManager","url":"https://github.com/BornToBeRoot","imageURL":"https://github.com/BornToBeRoot.png","key":"borntoberoot","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"introducing-neighbor-table","title":"Introducing Neighbor Table","description":"NETworkManager introduces the Neighbor Table, replacing the ARP Table with a unified view that covers both IPv4 (ARP) and IPv6 (NDP) address-to-MAC mappings.","authors":["borntoberoot"],"tags":["neighbor table","arp","ndp","ipv6","refactoring"],"keywords":["NETworkManager","neighbor table","ARP table","ARP","NDP","IPv6","IPv4","MAC address","neighbor discovery","address resolution"]},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Import Profiles from Active Directory","permalink":"/NETworkManager/blog/import-profiles-from-active-directory"},"nextItem":{"title":"Introducing Firewall","permalink":"/NETworkManager/blog/introducing-firewall"}},"content":"NETworkManager introduces the new **Neighbor Table**, replacing the **ARP Table** with a unified view that covers IP-to-MAC address mappings for both IPv4 (ARP) and IPv6 (NDP) in a single place.\\n\\n![Neighbor Table](./neighbor-table.png)\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\n## Why the change?\\n\\nThe old ARP Table only surfaced IPv4 entries. Modern networks increasingly rely on IPv6, and the equivalent mechanism on IPv6 \u2013 the **Neighbor Discovery Protocol (NDP)** \u2013 was left out entirely.\\n\\nRather than adding a separate IPv6 view, NETworkManager now provides a single **Neighbor Table** that shows both IPv4 and IPv6 neighbors side by side, reflecting what the Windows networking stack actually maintains.\\n\\n## ARP vs. NDP \u2014 what\'s the difference?\\n\\n**IPv4 \u2013 ARP (Address Resolution Protocol)** is a layer-2 protocol that maps IPv4 addresses to MAC addresses. When a device needs to send data to an IPv4 address, it first checks the ARP cache. If no entry is found, it broadcasts an ARP request; the target replies with its MAC address and the entry is cached.\\n\\n**IPv6 \u2013 NDP (Neighbor Discovery Protocol)** fulfills the same purpose for IPv6. Instead of broadcasts, NDP uses ICMPv6 Neighbor Solicitation and Advertisement messages sent to a solicited-node multicast address \u2014 making it more efficient and multicast-friendly.\\n\\nBoth protocols are susceptible to spoofing/poisoning attacks that can manipulate cached mappings and lead to man-in-the-middle scenarios.\\n\\n## What the Neighbor Table shows\\n\\nEach row in the table represents one cached neighbor entry and includes:\\n\\n| Column | Description |\\n| --- | --- |\\n| **IP Address** | IPv4 or IPv6 address of the cached neighbor. |\\n| **Interface** | Human-readable name of the network interface (e.g. `Ethernet`, `Wi-Fi`). |\\n| **MAC Address** | Link-layer (MAC) address associated with the IP address. |\\n| **State** | Current reachability state (Reachable, Stale, Permanent, \u2026). |\\n| **Multicast** | Indicates whether the IP address is a multicast address. |\\n\\nYou can refresh the table at any time with `F5` or let it update automatically (**Automatically update every**), and right-click any row to copy or export individual values, or to delete the selected entry. **Delete table** removes all dynamic entries at once (permanent entries are kept).\\n\\n## Adding static entries\\n\\nYou can also add permanent static neighbor entries \u2014 useful for pinning a critical device\'s MAC address so that ARP/NDP spoofing cannot redirect its traffic.\\n\\n![Add entry](./neighbor-table-entry.png)\\n\\nClick **Add entry...** below the table, supply an IPv4 or IPv6 address, the target MAC address, and the interface \u2014 NETworkManager takes care of the rest using `New-NetNeighbor` under the hood.\\n\\n:::note\\n\\nAdding and deleting neighbor entries requires administrator privileges. If NETworkManager is not running as administrator, the view is in read-only mode. Use the **Restart as administrator** button to relaunch with elevated rights.\\n\\n:::\\n\\n## Try it now\\n\\nThe Neighbor Table is available in the [latest version of NETworkManager](https://borntoberoot.net/NETworkManager/download).\\n\\nMore details are available in the [official documentation](https://borntoberoot.net/NETworkManager/docs/application/neighbor-table).\\n\\nIf you find any issues or have suggestions, please open an [issue on GitHub](https://github.com/BornToBeRoot/NETworkManager/issues)."},{"id":"introducing-firewall","metadata":{"permalink":"/NETworkManager/blog/introducing-firewall","editUrl":"https://github.com/bornToBeRoot/NETworkManager/tree/main/Website/blog/2026-04-30-introducing-firewall/index.md","source":"@site/blog/2026-04-30-introducing-firewall/index.md","title":"Introducing Firewall","description":"NETworkManager introduces the new Firewall feature. View, add, edit, enable, disable, and delete Windows Firewall rules managed by NETworkManager \u2014 all from a user-friendly interface.","date":"2026-04-30T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"firewall","permalink":"/NETworkManager/blog/tags/firewall"},{"inline":true,"label":"windows firewall","permalink":"/NETworkManager/blog/tags/windows-firewall"},{"inline":true,"label":"security","permalink":"/NETworkManager/blog/tags/security"},{"inline":true,"label":"new feature","permalink":"/NETworkManager/blog/tags/new-feature"}],"readingTime":2.76,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"BornToBeRoot","title":"Maintainer of NETworkManager","url":"https://github.com/BornToBeRoot","imageURL":"https://github.com/BornToBeRoot.png","key":"borntoberoot","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"introducing-firewall","title":"Introducing Firewall","description":"NETworkManager introduces the new Firewall feature. View, add, edit, enable, disable, and delete Windows Firewall rules managed by NETworkManager \u2014 all from a user-friendly interface.","authors":["borntoberoot"],"tags":["firewall","windows firewall","security","new feature"],"keywords":["NETworkManager","Windows Firewall","Windows Defender Firewall","firewall rules","inbound","outbound","network security"]},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Introducing Neighbor Table","permalink":"/NETworkManager/blog/introducing-neighbor-table"},"nextItem":{"title":"Deep Dive: Fixing DPI Scaling for Embedded Processes in WPF","permalink":"/NETworkManager/blog/deep-dive-fixing-dpi-scaling-for-embedded-processes-in-wpf"}},"content":"NETworkManager introduces a new feature, the `Firewall`. You can now manage Windows Firewall rules created by NETworkManager directly from within the app \u2014 no more jumping between MMC snap-ins for day-to-day tasks.\\n\\nThis is especially useful if you frequently spin up local services (lab environments, dev boxes, game servers, small internal tools) and need a quick and repeatable way to open or block ports, restrict traffic to specific IP ranges, or scope rules to profiles like **Domain**, **Private**, or **Public**.\\n\\n![Firewall](./firewall.png)\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\n## Manage rules safely (and without touching your system rules)\\n\\nBy default, the Firewall view focuses on rules managed by NETworkManager.\\n\\nEvery rule created via NETworkManager is stored with a `NETworkManager_` prefix in the Windows Firewall rule display name. This makes it easy to distinguish \\"your\\" rules from system-managed or third-party rules \u2014 and it allows NETworkManager to filter the list so you only see what it owns.\\n\\nIf you need the full picture, enable **Show all rules** in **Settings > Firewall**: all active Windows Firewall rules are shown, with an icon that tells whether a rule is managed by NETworkManager, by another application or by a policy (Group Policy / MDM). Rules of other applications can be enabled, disabled or deleted, rules from a policy can\'t be changed locally - and every rule can be used as a template for a new one.\\n\\n## What you can do with the new Firewall feature\\n\\n- View firewall rules created by NETworkManager (or all rules, see above)\\n- Add new inbound or outbound rules\\n- Edit existing rules (also with a double-click)\\n- Create a new rule based on an existing one with **Copy as...**\\n- Enable, disable or delete one or several rules at once\\n- Search by name, program, ports, addresses and more\\n- Copy or export rule information\\n- Refresh the list with `F5`\\n- Open the native Windows Firewall console (`WF.msc`) via the **Windows Firewall Settings** button\\n\\n## Add / Edit rules \u2014 with the options you actually need\\n\\nWhen creating or editing a rule, NETworkManager exposes the most common and important fields in a clear dialog:\\n\\n![Add rule](./firewall-rule.png)\\n\\n- **Name**: Display name of the rule (the `NETworkManager_` prefix is added automatically and hidden in the UI)\\n- **Enabled**: Whether the rule is active right after creation\\n- **Description**: Optional description of the rule\\n- **Direction**: Inbound / Outbound\\n- **Action**: Allow / Block\\n- **Protocol**: Any, TCP, UDP, ICMPv4, ICMPv6, GRE, L2TP\\n- **Local / Remote ports**: Available for TCP and UDP; multiple ports and ranges separated by `;`\\n- **Local / Remote addresses**: Supports single IPs, ranges and subnets (CIDR and subnet masks); remote addresses also keywords such as `LocalSubnet` or `Internet`\\n- **Program**: Limit the rule to a specific executable (optional, select it with a file dialog)\\n- **Interface type**: Any, Wired, Wireless, RemoteAccess\\n- **Network profiles**: Domain / Private / Public (at least one must be selected)\\n\\nYou can find all details (including examples for port and address formats) in the [official documentation](https://borntoberoot.net/NETworkManager/docs/application/firewall).\\n\\n## Administrator privileges\\n\\nManaging firewall rules requires elevated rights. If NETworkManager is not running as administrator, the Firewall view is **read-only**. Use the **Restart as administrator** button to relaunch NETworkManager with the required privileges.\\n\\n## Try it now\\n\\nThe Firewall is available in the [latest version of NETworkManager](https://borntoberoot.net/NETworkManager/download).\\n\\nIf you find any issues or have suggestions for improvement, please open an [issue on GitHub](https://github.com/BornToBeRoot/NETworkManager/issues)."},{"id":"deep-dive-fixing-dpi-scaling-for-embedded-processes-in-wpf","metadata":{"permalink":"/NETworkManager/blog/deep-dive-fixing-dpi-scaling-for-embedded-processes-in-wpf","editUrl":"https://github.com/bornToBeRoot/NETworkManager/tree/main/Website/blog/2026-03-04-deep-dive-fixing-dpi-scaling-for-embedded-processes-in-wpf/index.md","source":"@site/blog/2026-03-04-deep-dive-fixing-dpi-scaling-for-embedded-processes-in-wpf/index.md","title":"Deep Dive: Fixing DPI Scaling for Embedded Processes in WPF","description":"How NETworkManager fixes DPI scaling for embedded PowerShell and PuTTY windows in WPF using the Console API and WM_DPICHANGED.","date":"2026-03-04T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"wpf","permalink":"/NETworkManager/blog/tags/wpf"},{"inline":true,"label":"dpi","permalink":"/NETworkManager/blog/tags/dpi"},{"inline":true,"label":"windows","permalink":"/NETworkManager/blog/tags/windows"},{"inline":true,"label":"c#","permalink":"/NETworkManager/blog/tags/c"},{"inline":true,"label":"win32","permalink":"/NETworkManager/blog/tags/win-32"},{"inline":true,"label":"putty","permalink":"/NETworkManager/blog/tags/putty"},{"inline":true,"label":"powershell","permalink":"/NETworkManager/blog/tags/powershell"}],"readingTime":6.59,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"BornToBeRoot","title":"Maintainer of NETworkManager","url":"https://github.com/BornToBeRoot","imageURL":"https://github.com/BornToBeRoot.png","key":"borntoberoot","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"deep-dive-fixing-dpi-scaling-for-embedded-processes-in-wpf","title":"Deep Dive: Fixing DPI Scaling for Embedded Processes in WPF","description":"How NETworkManager fixes DPI scaling for embedded PowerShell and PuTTY windows in WPF using the Console API and WM_DPICHANGED.","authors":["borntoberoot"],"tags":["wpf","dpi","windows","c#","win32","putty","powershell"],"keywords":["WPF","DPI scaling","SetParent","PowerShell","PuTTY","Win32","WM_DPICHANGED","high DPI","multi-monitor","C#"]},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Introducing Firewall","permalink":"/NETworkManager/blog/introducing-firewall"},"nextItem":{"title":"System-Wide Policies for Enterprise Deployments","permalink":"/NETworkManager/blog/system-wide-policies-for-enterprise-deployments"}},"content":"Modern Windows setups with multiple monitors at different scale factors (100 %, 125 %, 150 %, \u2026) expose a hard Win32 limitation the moment you embed a foreign process window into your own application via `SetParent`. Windows simply does not forward `WM_DPICHANGED` across process boundaries.\\n\\nThis article documents the investigation and the two different solutions NETworkManager uses for its embedded **PowerShell** (a console host process) and **PuTTY** (a GUI process) tabs \u2014 together with the complete, relevant C# source code.\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\n## The Embedding Technique\\n\\nNETworkManager is a C#/WPF application that uses `WindowsFormsHost` to host a native Win32 `Panel` (WinForms `Panel`), and then calls `SetParent` to re-parent a foreign process window into that panel.\\n\\nThe XAML wires up the `DpiChanged` event and embeds a WinForms `Panel` as the hosting surface:\\n\\n```xml\\n<WindowsFormsHost DpiChanged=\\"WindowsFormsHost_DpiChanged\\">\\n    <windowsForms:Panel x:Name=\\"WindowHost\\" />\\n</WindowsFormsHost>\\n```\\n\\nThe C# code-behind then calls `SetParent` to embed the external process window:\\n\\n```csharp\\n// Make the external process window a child of our WinForms panel\\nNativeMethods.SetParent(_appWin, WindowHost.Handle);\\n\\n// Strip decorations so it looks native inside our tab\\nlong style = (int)NativeMethods.GetWindowLong(_appWin, NativeMethods.GWL_STYLE);\\nstyle &= ~(NativeMethods.WS_CAPTION | NativeMethods.WS_POPUP | NativeMethods.WS_THICKFRAME);\\nNativeMethods.SetWindowLongPtr(_appWin, NativeMethods.GWL_STYLE, new IntPtr(style));\\n```\\n\\nThis works fine visually \u2014 the external window appears seamlessly inside the WPF application. **But fonts never rescale when the user drags the window to a monitor with a different DPI.**\\n\\n## Why DPI Notifications Break\\n\\nWPF applications declare `PerMonitorV2` DPI awareness in their manifest. When the application\'s `HwndSource` (the root Win32 window) moves to a different DPI monitor, Windows walks the entire Win32 window tree within the **same process** and sends `WM_DPICHANGED` / `WM_DPICHANGED_AFTERPARENT` to every child. The `WindowsFormsHost` \u2192 `WindowHost` chain is all in-process, so it receives `DpiChanged` events correctly.\\n\\nThe problem is that `_appWin` is owned by a **completely separate process** (PuTTY, conhost). From the Windows DWM compositor\'s perspective it is now a child window of your panel, but the DPI notification system only walks intra-process window trees. The external child window never receives any DPI message.\\n\\n## Solution A \u2014 Console Host Processes (PowerShell, cmd)\\n\\nPowerShell runs inside **conhost.exe**, the Windows console host. Unlike a GUI process, conhost exposes its font settings through the Console API (`kernel32.dll`). This is a true cross-process interface: any process can attach to an existing console and modify its font without sending any window messages.\\n\\n```csharp\\n// NativeMethods helpers used below\\n[DllImport(\\"kernel32.dll\\", SetLastError = true)]\\npublic static extern bool AttachConsole(uint dwProcessId);\\n\\n[DllImport(\\"kernel32.dll\\", SetLastError = true)]\\npublic static extern bool FreeConsole();\\n\\n[DllImport(\\"kernel32.dll\\", SetLastError = true, CharSet = CharSet.Unicode)]\\nprivate static extern IntPtr CreateFile(string lpFileName, uint dwDesiredAccess,\\n    uint dwShareMode, IntPtr lpSecurityAttributes, uint dwCreationDisposition,\\n    uint dwFlagsAndAttributes, IntPtr hTemplateFile);\\n\\n[DllImport(\\"kernel32.dll\\", CharSet = CharSet.Unicode, SetLastError = true)]\\nprivate static extern bool GetCurrentConsoleFontEx(IntPtr hConsoleOutput,\\n    bool bMaximumWindow, ref CONSOLE_FONT_INFOEX lpConsoleCurrentFontEx);\\n\\n[DllImport(\\"kernel32.dll\\", CharSet = CharSet.Unicode, SetLastError = true)]\\nprivate static extern bool SetCurrentConsoleFontEx(IntPtr hConsoleOutput,\\n    bool bMaximumWindow, ref CONSOLE_FONT_INFOEX lpConsoleCurrentFontEx);\\n```\\n\\n### Font rescale helper\\n\\n```csharp\\n/// <summary>\\n/// Attaches to <paramref name=\\"processId\\"/>\'s console and rescales its current font\\n/// by <paramref name=\\"scaleFactor\\"/> using SetCurrentConsoleFontEx.\\n/// Works for any conhost-based console (PowerShell, cmd, etc.).\\n/// </summary>\\npublic static void TryRescaleConsoleFont(uint processId, double scaleFactor)\\n{\\n    if (Math.Abs(scaleFactor - 1.0) < 0.01)\\n        return;\\n\\n    if (!AttachConsole(processId))\\n        return;\\n\\n    const uint GENERIC_READ_WRITE = 0xC0000000u;\\n    const uint FILE_SHARE_READ_WRITE = 3u;\\n    const uint OPEN_EXISTING = 3u;\\n\\n    var hOut = CreateFile(\\"CONOUT$\\", GENERIC_READ_WRITE, FILE_SHARE_READ_WRITE,\\n        IntPtr.Zero, OPEN_EXISTING, 0u, IntPtr.Zero);\\n    try\\n    {\\n        if (hOut == INVALID_HANDLE_VALUE)\\n            return;\\n        try\\n        {\\n            var fi = new CONSOLE_FONT_INFOEX\\n            {\\n                cbSize = (uint)Marshal.SizeOf<CONSOLE_FONT_INFOEX>()\\n            };\\n            if (GetCurrentConsoleFontEx(hOut, false, ref fi))\\n            {\\n                fi.dwFontSize.Y = (short)Math.Max(1,\\n                    (int)Math.Round(fi.dwFontSize.Y * scaleFactor));\\n                fi.cbSize = (uint)Marshal.SizeOf<CONSOLE_FONT_INFOEX>();\\n                SetCurrentConsoleFontEx(hOut, false, ref fi);\\n            }\\n        }\\n        finally { CloseHandle(hOut); }\\n    }\\n    finally { FreeConsole(); }\\n}\\n```\\n\\n### Using it in the PowerShell control\\n\\n`WindowsFormsHost` raises `DpiChanged` when the parent WPF window moves monitors. The handler uses the `NewDpi / OldDpi` ratio to rescale the font relatively:\\n\\n```csharp\\nprivate void WindowsFormsHost_DpiChanged(object sender, DpiChangedEventArgs e)\\n{\\n    ResizeEmbeddedWindow();\\n\\n    if (!IsConnected)\\n        return;\\n\\n    // Rescale the console font using the new/old DPI ratio via the Console API.\\n    // WM_DPICHANGED is never forwarded to cross-process child windows,\\n    // so we use AttachConsole + SetCurrentConsoleFontEx instead.\\n    NativeMethods.TryRescaleConsoleFont(\\n        (uint)_process.Id,\\n        e.NewDpi.PixelsPerInchX / e.OldDpi.PixelsPerInchX);\\n}\\n```\\n\\n### Fixing the initial DPI baseline\\n\\nThere is a subtle bug if the embedded process spawns on a **different monitor** than NETworkManager: conhost\'s font is scaled for *its* monitor\'s DPI, not ours. Every subsequent `newDpi/oldDpi` relative rescale will then compound the error.\\n\\nFix: read `GetDpiForWindow` for both windows **before** `SetParent`, and correct the baseline immediately after embedding:\\n\\n```csharp\\n// Capture DPI before embedding to correct font scaling afterwards\\nvar initialWindowDpi = NativeMethods.GetDpiForWindow(_appWin);\\n\\nNativeMethods.SetParent(_appWin, WindowHost.Handle);\\n// ... ShowWindow, strip styles, IsConnected = true ...\\n\\nawait Task.Delay(250);\\nResizeEmbeddedWindow();\\n\\n// Correct font if conhost started at a different DPI than our panel\\nvar currentPanelDpi = NativeMethods.GetDpiForWindow(WindowHost.Handle);\\nif (initialWindowDpi != currentPanelDpi)\\n    NativeMethods.TryRescaleConsoleFont(\\n        (uint)_process.Id,\\n        (double)currentPanelDpi / initialWindowDpi);\\n```\\n\\n`GetDpiForWindow` is available since Windows 10 version 1607:\\n\\n```csharp\\n[DllImport(\\"user32.dll\\")]\\npublic static extern uint GetDpiForWindow(IntPtr hWnd);\\n```\\n\\n## Solution B \u2014 GUI Processes (PuTTY)\\n\\nPuTTY is a standard Win32 GUI application, not a console. The Console API does not apply. Instead, the approach is to send `WM_DPICHANGED` (0x02E0) directly to the PuTTY window, which it handles natively (requires PuTTY 0.75+ for reliable behaviour).\\n\\n`WM_DPICHANGED` carries the new DPI packed into `wParam` (LOWORD = DPI X, HIWORD = DPI Y) and a `RECT*` in `lParam` with the suggested new window rect:\\n\\n### DPI message helper\\n\\n```csharp\\n/// <summary>\\n/// Sends WM_DPICHANGED to a GUI window so it can rescale its fonts and layout.\\n/// WM_DPICHANGED is not reliably forwarded to cross-process child windows\\n/// embedded via SetParent, so we send it explicitly.\\n/// </summary>\\npublic static void TrySendDpiChangedMessage(IntPtr hWnd, double oldDpi, double newDpi)\\n{\\n    if (hWnd == IntPtr.Zero)\\n        return;\\n\\n    if (Math.Abs(newDpi - oldDpi) < 0.01)\\n        return;\\n\\n    const uint WM_DPICHANGED = 0x02E0;\\n\\n    var newDpiInt = (int)Math.Round(newDpi);\\n    // HIWORD = Y DPI, LOWORD = X DPI\\n    var wParam = (IntPtr)((newDpiInt << 16) | newDpiInt);\\n\\n    // lParam must point to a RECT with the suggested new size/position.\\n    var rect = new RECT();\\n    GetWindowRect(hWnd, ref rect);\\n\\n    var lParam = Marshal.AllocHGlobal(Marshal.SizeOf<RECT>());\\n    try\\n    {\\n        Marshal.StructureToPtr(rect, lParam, false);\\n        SendMessage(hWnd, WM_DPICHANGED, wParam, lParam);\\n    }\\n    finally\\n    {\\n        Marshal.FreeHGlobal(lParam);\\n    }\\n}\\n```\\n\\n### Using it in the PuTTY control\\n\\n```csharp\\nprivate void WindowsFormsHost_DpiChanged(object sender, DpiChangedEventArgs e)\\n{\\n    ResizeEmbeddedWindow();\\n\\n    if (!IsConnected)\\n        return;\\n\\n    // Send WM_DPICHANGED explicitly to the PuTTY window with the new DPI.\\n    // WM_DPICHANGED is never forwarded to cross-process child windows after SetParent,\\n    // so we inject the message directly.\\n    NativeMethods.TrySendDpiChangedMessage(\\n        _appWin,\\n        e.OldDpi.PixelsPerInchX,\\n        e.NewDpi.PixelsPerInchX);\\n}\\n```\\n\\n### Fixing the initial DPI baseline\\n\\nSame issue as PowerShell: PuTTY may start on a different monitor. Because PuTTY is a GUI process, the console API does not apply \u2014 but the explicit `WM_DPICHANGED` message works for the initial correction too:\\n\\n```csharp\\n// Capture DPI before embedding\\nvar initialWindowDpi = NativeMethods.GetDpiForWindow(_appWin);\\n\\nNativeMethods.SetParent(_appWin, WindowHost.Handle);\\n// ... ShowWindow, strip styles, IsConnected = true ...\\n\\nawait Task.Delay(250);\\nResizeEmbeddedWindow();\\n\\n// Correct DPI if PuTTY started at a different DPI than our panel\\nvar currentPanelDpi = NativeMethods.GetDpiForWindow(WindowHost.Handle);\\nif (initialWindowDpi != currentPanelDpi)\\n    NativeMethods.TrySendDpiChangedMessage(_appWin, initialWindowDpi, currentPanelDpi);\\n```\\n\\n## Sizing the WindowsFormsHost at Load\\n\\nOne more pitfall: `WindowsFormsHost` starts with zero size because WPF\'s logical pixel coordinates do not account for the system DPI. The panel\'s `ClientSize` must be set in **physical pixels**:\\n\\n```csharp\\nprivate void UserControl_Loaded(object sender, RoutedEventArgs e)\\n{\\n    if (_initialized) return;\\n\\n    // VisualTreeHelper.GetDpi returns DpiScaleX/Y as a ratio (1.0 = 96 DPI, 1.5 = 144 DPI).\\n    var dpi = System.Windows.Media.VisualTreeHelper.GetDpi(this);\\n    WindowHost.Height = (int)(ActualHeight * dpi.DpiScaleY);\\n    WindowHost.Width  = (int)(ActualWidth  * dpi.DpiScaleX);\\n\\n    Connect().ConfigureAwait(false);\\n    _initialized = true;\\n}\\n```\\n\\n## Summary\\n\\nWhen you embed a foreign process window via `SetParent`, Windows never forwards DPI change notifications across process boundaries. For console host processes (PowerShell, cmd) use the Windows Console API (`AttachConsole` + `SetCurrentConsoleFontEx`) to rescale fonts directly; for GUI processes (PuTTY) send `WM_DPICHANGED` (0x02E0) explicitly with the new DPI packed into `wParam`. In both cases, apply an initial DPI correction after `SetParent` by comparing `GetDpiForWindow` before and after embedding, and set the `WindowsFormsHost` initial size in physical pixels using `VisualTreeHelper.GetDpi`.\\n\\nThe full implementation is available in the NETworkManager source:\\n\\n- [`NETworkManager.Utilities/NativeMethods.cs`](https://github.com/BornToBeRoot/NETworkManager/blob/main/Source/NETworkManager.Utilities/NativeMethods.cs) \u2014 all P/Invoke declarations and helpers\\n- [`NETworkManager/Controls/PowerShellControl.xaml.cs`](https://github.com/BornToBeRoot/NETworkManager/blob/main/Source/NETworkManager/Controls/PowerShellControl.xaml.cs) \u2014 console host approach\\n- [`NETworkManager/Controls/PuTTYControl.xaml.cs`](https://github.com/BornToBeRoot/NETworkManager/blob/main/Source/NETworkManager/Controls/PuTTYControl.xaml.cs) \u2014 GUI process approach"},{"id":"system-wide-policies-for-enterprise-deployments","metadata":{"permalink":"/NETworkManager/blog/system-wide-policies-for-enterprise-deployments","editUrl":"https://github.com/bornToBeRoot/NETworkManager/tree/main/Website/blog/2026-02-22-system-wide-policies-for-enterprise-deployments/index.md","source":"@site/blog/2026-02-22-system-wide-policies-for-enterprise-deployments/index.md","title":"System-Wide Policies for Enterprise Deployments","description":"NETworkManager now supports system-wide policies for centralized control over settings across all users \u2014 ideal for enterprise deployments.","date":"2026-02-22T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"policies","permalink":"/NETworkManager/blog/tags/policies"},{"inline":true,"label":"enterprise","permalink":"/NETworkManager/blog/tags/enterprise"},{"inline":true,"label":"settings","permalink":"/NETworkManager/blog/tags/settings"},{"inline":true,"label":"new feature","permalink":"/NETworkManager/blog/tags/new-feature"}],"readingTime":3.3,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"BornToBeRoot","title":"Maintainer of NETworkManager","url":"https://github.com/BornToBeRoot","imageURL":"https://github.com/BornToBeRoot.png","key":"borntoberoot","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"system-wide-policies-for-enterprise-deployments","title":"System-Wide Policies for Enterprise Deployments","description":"NETworkManager now supports system-wide policies for centralized control over settings across all users \u2014 ideal for enterprise deployments.","authors":["borntoberoot"],"tags":["policies","enterprise","settings","new feature"],"keywords":["NETworkManager","system-wide policies","enterprise","deployment","Intune","SCCM","Group Policy","configuration management"]},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Deep Dive: Fixing DPI Scaling for Embedded Processes in WPF","permalink":"/NETworkManager/blog/deep-dive-fixing-dpi-scaling-for-embedded-processes-in-wpf"},"nextItem":{"title":"Improved Light Theme Readability","permalink":"/NETworkManager/blog/improved-light-theme-readability"}},"content":"NETworkManager now supports system-wide policies, giving administrators centralized control over application settings across all users on a machine.\\n\\n![System-wide policy indicator](./system-wide-policy-indicator.png)\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\n## What Are System-Wide Policies?\\n\\nSystem-wide policies allow administrators to enforce specific settings for all users on a machine. These policies override user-specific settings and provide centralized control over application behavior in enterprise environments.\\n\\nWhen a policy is active, the corresponding setting is locked in the UI and displays a shield icon along with a message indicating that the setting is managed by an administrator. Users can see the enforced value but cannot change it.\\n\\n## How to Configure Policies\\n\\nPolicies are defined in a `config.json` file placed in the application installation directory (the same folder as `NETworkManager.exe`). When this file exists, the application loads the policies at startup and applies them with precedence over user settings. An example file (`config.json.example`) is included in the application installation directory for reference.\\n\\n**File location:**\\n\\n- **Installed version**: `C:\\\\Program Files\\\\NETworkManager\\\\config.json` (or your custom installation path)\\n- **Portable version**: Same directory as `NETworkManager.exe`\\n\\n**File format:**\\n\\n```json\\n{\\n  \\"Policy_Name\\": true\\n}\\n```\\n\\nProperty names follow the pattern `Section_SettingName`. You can find the available policy names and values in the corresponding [setting\'s documentation](https://borntoberoot.net/NETworkManager/docs/category/settings).\\n\\n### Example Policy\\n\\nFor example, the [`Update_CheckForUpdatesAtStartup`](https://borntoberoot.net/NETworkManager/docs/settings/update) policy controls whether the application checks for new program versions on GitHub when the application is launched. The [`Profiles_FolderLocation`](https://borntoberoot.net/NETworkManager/docs/settings/profiles) and [`Settings_FolderLocation`](https://borntoberoot.net/NETworkManager/docs/settings/settings) policies allow administrators to redirect profile and settings storage to a centralized location, such as a network share or a custom local path.\\n\\n**Values:**\\n\\n- `Update_CheckForUpdatesAtStartup`:\\n  - `true` \u2014 Force enable automatic update checks at startup for all users\\n  - `false` \u2014 Force disable automatic update checks at startup for all users\\n  - Omit the property \u2014 Allow users to control this setting themselves\\n- `Profiles_FolderLocation` / `Settings_FolderLocation`:\\n  - Absolute path (e.g., `C:\\\\\\\\Path\\\\\\\\To\\\\\\\\Profiles`)\\n  - Path with environment variables (e.g., `%UserProfile%\\\\\\\\NETworkManager\\\\\\\\Settings`)\\n  - UNC path (e.g., `\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\Server\\\\\\\\Shared\\\\\\\\NETworkManager\\\\\\\\Profiles`)\\n  - Omit the property \u2014 Allow the default location logic to apply\\n\\n**Example `config.json`:**\\n\\n```json\\n{\\n  \\"Update_CheckForUpdatesAtStartup\\": false,\\n  \\"Profiles_FolderLocation\\": \\"\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\Server\\\\\\\\Shared\\\\\\\\NETworkManager\\\\\\\\Profiles\\",\\n  \\"Settings_FolderLocation\\": \\"%UserProfile%\\\\\\\\NETworkManager\\\\\\\\Settings\\"\\n}\\n```\\n\\n:::note\\n\\n- The file must be named exactly `config.json`\\n- The file must contain valid JSON syntax\\n- Changes to the file require restarting the application to take effect\\n- If the file doesn\'t exist or contains invalid JSON, it will be ignored and user settings will apply\\n\\n:::\\n\\n## Deploying Policies\\n\\n1. **Create the configuration file** \u2014 Use the `config.json.example` as a template, rename it to `config.json`, and set your desired policy values.\\n\\n2. **Deploy to installation directory** \u2014 Place the `config.json` file in the same directory as `NETworkManager.exe`. For MSI installations, this is typically `C:\\\\Program Files\\\\NETworkManager\\\\`.\\n\\n3. **Deploy methods:**\\n   - Group Policy \u2014 copy the file to the installation directory (use Group Policy preferences or a startup script)\\n   - Configuration management tools \u2014 SCCM/ConfigMgr, Microsoft Intune, Ansible, etc.\\n   - Scripts and deployment toolkits \u2014 PowerShell scripts, PSAppDeployToolkit\\n   - Manual deployment \u2014 hand-copy for small-scale rollouts\\n\\n4. **Verification:**\\n   - Launch the application\\n   - Navigate to Settings > Update (e.g., \\"Check for updates at startup\\")\\n   - Verify the shield icon and the administrator message appear and that the control is disabled\\n\\n:::warning\\n\\nEnsure the `config.json` file has appropriate permissions so that regular users cannot modify it. On standard installations in `Program Files`, this is automatically enforced by Windows file permissions.\\n\\n:::\\n\\n## Request More Policies\\n\\nAdditional policy options will be added in future releases to provide more granular control over application behavior. If you have specific requirements for system-wide policies in your organization, please submit a feature request via the [GitHub issue tracker](https://github.com/BornToBeRoot/NETworkManager/issues/new/choose) to help us prioritize.\\n\\nUpgrade now to the [latest version of NETworkManager](https://borntoberoot.net/NETworkManager/download) to use this feature.\\n\\nMore information is available in the [official documentation](https://borntoberoot.net/NETworkManager/docs/system-wide-policies).\\n\\nIf you find any issues or have suggestions for improvement, please open an [issue on GitHub](https://github.com/BornToBeRoot/NETworkManager/issues)."},{"id":"improved-light-theme-readability","metadata":{"permalink":"/NETworkManager/blog/improved-light-theme-readability","editUrl":"https://github.com/bornToBeRoot/NETworkManager/tree/main/Website/blog/2025-10-23-improved-light-theme-readability/index.md","source":"@site/blog/2025-10-23-improved-light-theme-readability/index.md","title":"Improved Light Theme Readability","description":"NETworkManager 2025.10.18.0 brings major enhancements to the light theme with improved readability, contrast, and overall user experience.","date":"2025-10-23T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"theme","permalink":"/NETworkManager/blog/tags/theme"},{"inline":true,"label":"ui","permalink":"/NETworkManager/blog/tags/ui"}],"readingTime":0.8,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"BornToBeRoot","title":"Maintainer of NETworkManager","url":"https://github.com/BornToBeRoot","imageURL":"https://github.com/BornToBeRoot.png","key":"borntoberoot","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"improved-light-theme-readability","title":"Improved Light Theme Readability","description":"NETworkManager 2025.10.18.0 brings major enhancements to the light theme with improved readability, contrast, and overall user experience.","authors":["borntoberoot"],"tags":["theme","ui"],"keywords":["NETworkManager","light theme","dark theme","readability","UI","accessibility","theming"]},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"System-Wide Policies for Enterprise Deployments","permalink":"/NETworkManager/blog/system-wide-policies-for-enterprise-deployments"},"nextItem":{"title":"Streamlined Profile Management with Tags & Filters","permalink":"/NETworkManager/blog/streamlined-profile-management-with-tags-and-filters"}},"content":"The latest release of NETworkManager (version 2025.10.18.0) introduces major enhancements to the light theme, improving both readability and overall user experience.\\n\\nIn this version, the code that handles theming has been completely rewritten, providing granular control over colors and styles. This allows users to fine-tune the light and dark themes independently, ensuring better contrast, visibility, and accessibility across the interface.\\n\\nThe light theme has already received several improvements to enhance readability, and more adjustments are planned for future releases based on user feedback. So please don\u2019t hesitate to share your thoughts on the [GitHub issue tracker](https://github.com/BornToBeRoot/NETworkManager/issues).\\n\\n![Preview - Light theme](./preview-light-theme.png)\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\nUpgrade now to the [latest version of NETworkManager](https://borntoberoot.net/NETworkManager/download) to use this feature.\\n\\nMore information is available in the [official documentation](https://borntoberoot.net/NETworkManager/docs/settings/appearance).\\n\\nIf you find any issues or have suggestions for improvement, please open an [issue on GitHub](https://github.com/BornToBeRoot/NETworkManager/issues)."},{"id":"streamlined-profile-management-with-tags-and-filters","metadata":{"permalink":"/NETworkManager/blog/streamlined-profile-management-with-tags-and-filters","editUrl":"https://github.com/bornToBeRoot/NETworkManager/tree/main/Website/blog/2025-10-19-streamlined-profile-management-with-tags-and-filters/index.md","source":"@site/blog/2025-10-19-streamlined-profile-management-with-tags-and-filters/index.md","title":"Streamlined Profile Management with Tags & Filters","description":"NETworkManager 2025.10.18.0 introduces tags and filtering for profiles. Add or remove tags to organize and quickly locate your hosts and networks.","date":"2025-10-19T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"profile management","permalink":"/NETworkManager/blog/tags/profile-management"},{"inline":true,"label":"tags","permalink":"/NETworkManager/blog/tags/tags"},{"inline":true,"label":"new feature","permalink":"/NETworkManager/blog/tags/new-feature"}],"readingTime":0.95,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"BornToBeRoot","title":"Maintainer of NETworkManager","url":"https://github.com/BornToBeRoot","imageURL":"https://github.com/BornToBeRoot.png","key":"borntoberoot","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"streamlined-profile-management-with-tags-and-filters","title":"Streamlined Profile Management with Tags & Filters","description":"NETworkManager 2025.10.18.0 introduces tags and filtering for profiles. Add or remove tags to organize and quickly locate your hosts and networks.","authors":["borntoberoot"],"tags":["profile management","tags","new feature"],"keywords":["NETworkManager","profile management","tags","filters","network profiles","host management"]},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Improved Light Theme Readability","permalink":"/NETworkManager/blog/improved-light-theme-readability"},"nextItem":{"title":"Introducing Hosts File Editor","permalink":"/NETworkManager/blog/introducing-hosts-file-editor"}},"content":"NETworkManager 2025.10.18.0 brings a streamlined approach to profile management with the introduction of Tags and Filtering.\\n\\nYou can now add or remove tags directly within each profile, making it effortless to organize and quickly locate your hosts and networks.\\n\\n![Profile Tags & Filters](./profile-tags-and-filters.gif)\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\nEasily filter profiles across features using the new filter button next to the search box.\\n\\nChoose between two filter modes:\\n\\n- Match All: Display profiles containing all selected tags (e.g., `prod` and `dns`).\\n- Match Any: Display profiles containing at least one selected tag (e.g., `prod` or `dev`).\\n\\nAdditionally, profile groups are now more manageable. Right-click any group header to access a new context menu, allowing you to expand or collapse all groups with a single click.\\n\\nThese enhancements make managing large profile sets faster, cleaner, and more intuitive than ever.\\n\\nUpgrade now to the [latest version of NETworkManager](https://borntoberoot.net/NETworkManager/download) to use this feature.\\n\\nMore information is available in the [official documentation](https://borntoberoot.net/NETworkManager/docs/groups-and-profiles).\\n\\nIf you find any issues or have suggestions for improvement, please open an [issue on GitHub](https://github.com/BornToBeRoot/NETworkManager/issues)."},{"id":"introducing-hosts-file-editor","metadata":{"permalink":"/NETworkManager/blog/introducing-hosts-file-editor","editUrl":"https://github.com/bornToBeRoot/NETworkManager/tree/main/Website/blog/2025-09-06-introducing-hosts-file-editor/index.md","source":"@site/blog/2025-09-06-introducing-hosts-file-editor/index.md","title":"Introducing Hosts File Editor","description":"NETworkManager 2025.8.10.0 introduces the Hosts File Editor. Easily manage and edit your system\'s hosts file in a user-friendly interface.","date":"2025-09-06T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"hosts file","permalink":"/NETworkManager/blog/tags/hosts-file"},{"inline":true,"label":"dns","permalink":"/NETworkManager/blog/tags/dns"},{"inline":true,"label":"new feature","permalink":"/NETworkManager/blog/tags/new-feature"}],"readingTime":1.11,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"BornToBeRoot","title":"Maintainer of NETworkManager","url":"https://github.com/BornToBeRoot","imageURL":"https://github.com/BornToBeRoot.png","key":"borntoberoot","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"introducing-hosts-file-editor","title":"Introducing Hosts File Editor","description":"NETworkManager 2025.8.10.0 introduces the Hosts File Editor. Easily manage and edit your system\'s hosts file in a user-friendly interface.","authors":["borntoberoot"],"tags":["hosts file","dns","new feature"],"keywords":["NETworkManager","hosts file","hosts editor","DNS","Windows","network tool"]},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Streamlined Profile Management with Tags & Filters","permalink":"/NETworkManager/blog/streamlined-profile-management-with-tags-and-filters"},"nextItem":{"title":"Introducing Code Signing for Binaries","permalink":"/NETworkManager/blog/introducing-code-signing-for-binaries"}},"content":"NETworkManager 2025.8.10.0 introduced a new feature, the `Hosts File Editor`. You can now easily manage and edit your system\'s hosts file in a user-friendly interface.\\n\\nAs a sysadmin or developer, you often need to modify the hosts file in order to override DNS settings for specific domains, redirect traffic, or test websites locally.\\n\\n![Hosts File Editor](./hosts-file-editor.gif)\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\nThe hosts file is a plain text file used by the operating system to map hostnames to IP addresses. It is typically located at:\\n\\n- Windows: `C:\\\\Windows\\\\System32\\\\drivers\\\\etc\\\\hosts`\\n- Linux: `/etc/hosts`\\n\\nEditing this file usually requires administrative privileges and can be cumbersome using traditional text editors.\\nWith the new Hosts File Editor in NETworkManager, you can now easily add, edit, enable, disable, or delete entries\\nin your hosts file without needing to manually open and edit the file.\\n\\nA daily backup (up to 5 versions) of the hosts file is created automatically before any changes are applied,\\nallowing you to restore previous versions if needed.\\n\\nUpgrade now to the [latest version of NETworkManager](https://borntoberoot.net/NETworkManager/download) to use this feature.\\n\\nMore information is available in the [official documentation](https://borntoberoot.net/NETworkManager/docs/application/hosts-file-editor).\\n\\nIf you find any issues or have suggestions for improvement, please open an [issue on GitHub](https://github.com/BornToBeRoot/NETworkManager/issues)."},{"id":"introducing-code-signing-for-binaries","metadata":{"permalink":"/NETworkManager/blog/introducing-code-signing-for-binaries","editUrl":"https://github.com/bornToBeRoot/NETworkManager/tree/main/Website/blog/2024-05-24-introducing-code-signing-to-binaries/index.md","source":"@site/blog/2024-05-24-introducing-code-signing-to-binaries/index.md","title":"Introducing Code Signing for Binaries","description":"Starting with NETworkManager version 2024.5.24.0, the binaries and the installer are now signed with a code signing certificate.","date":"2024-05-24T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"code-signing","permalink":"/NETworkManager/blog/tags/code-signing"},{"inline":true,"label":"binaries","permalink":"/NETworkManager/blog/tags/binaries"},{"inline":true,"label":"installer","permalink":"/NETworkManager/blog/tags/installer"}],"readingTime":0.53,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"BornToBeRoot","title":"Maintainer of NETworkManager","url":"https://github.com/BornToBeRoot","imageURL":"https://github.com/BornToBeRoot.png","key":"borntoberoot","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"introducing-code-signing-for-binaries","title":"Introducing Code Signing for Binaries","description":"Starting with NETworkManager version 2024.5.24.0, the binaries and the installer are now signed with a code signing certificate.","authors":["borntoberoot"],"tags":["code-signing","binaries","installer"],"keywords":["NETworkManager","code signing","binaries","installer","SignPath","certificate","security"]},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Introducing Hosts File Editor","permalink":"/NETworkManager/blog/introducing-hosts-file-editor"},"nextItem":{"title":"Welcome","permalink":"/NETworkManager/blog/welcome"}},"content":"Starting with NETworkManager version 2024.5.24.0, the binaries and the installer are now signed with a code signing certificate.\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\nThe binaries and installer are built on [AppVeyor](https://ci.appveyor.com/project/BornToBeRoot/networkmanager) directly from the [GitHub repository](https://github.com/BornToBeRoot/NETworkManager/blob/main/appveyor.yml).\\nBuild artifacts are automatically sent to [SignPath.io](https://signpath.io/) via webhook, where they are signed after manual approval by the maintainer.\\nThe signed binaries are then uploaded to the [GitHub releases](https://github.com/BornToBeRoot/NETworkManager/releases) page.\\n\\nSpecial thanks goes to [SignPath.io](https://signpath.io/) for providing free code signing and the [SignPath Foundation](https://signpath.org/) for the free code signing certificate."},{"id":"welcome","metadata":{"permalink":"/NETworkManager/blog/welcome","editUrl":"https://github.com/bornToBeRoot/NETworkManager/tree/main/Website/blog/2024-01-01-welcome/index.md","source":"@site/blog/2024-01-01-welcome/index.md","title":"Welcome","description":"Welcome to the new NETworkManager website, blog and documentation. It\'s built with Docusaurus, a static website generator, and hosted on GitHub Pages.","date":"2024-01-01T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"welcome","permalink":"/NETworkManager/blog/tags/welcome"},{"inline":true,"label":"docusaurus","permalink":"/NETworkManager/blog/tags/docusaurus"}],"readingTime":0.22,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"BornToBeRoot","title":"Maintainer of NETworkManager","url":"https://github.com/BornToBeRoot","imageURL":"https://github.com/BornToBeRoot.png","key":"borntoberoot","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"welcome","title":"Welcome","description":"Welcome to the new NETworkManager website, blog and documentation. It\'s built with Docusaurus, a static website generator, and hosted on GitHub Pages.","authors":["borntoberoot"],"tags":["welcome","docusaurus"],"keywords":["NETworkManager","website","blog","documentation","Docusaurus","GitHub Pages"]},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Introducing Code Signing for Binaries","permalink":"/NETworkManager/blog/introducing-code-signing-for-binaries"}},"content":"Welcome to the new NETworkManager website, blog and documentation.\\n\\nIt\'s built with [Docusaurus](https://docusaurus.io/), a static website generator, and hosted on [GitHub Pages](https://pages.github.com/).\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\nYou can help to improve the documentation by submitting a [pull request](https://github.com/BornToBeRoot/NETworkManager)."}]}}')}}]);